STAR-BRICK 01, Version 1.1: 

A Theorem-Driven Theory of Energy-Aware Scheduling for Space-Based Computing

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

This work develops a theorem-driven theory of energy-aware scheduling for a distributed computing infrastructure operating under coupled energy, thermal, availability, and communication constraints. The system is modeled as a discrete-time transition system x_{t+1} = f(x_t, a_t, w_t) over a state decomposed into a continuous component x^c = (B, T, C), comprising stored energy, temperature, and computing capacity, and a discrete component x^d = (A, Q, \mathcal{N}), comprising node availability, the task queue, and the communication graph. The discrete component is observed exactly; the continuous component is observed through an estimate \hat{x}^c_t satisfying \|x_t^c - \hat{x}_t^c\| \le \varepsilon. Because admissibility is checked against the estimate rather than the true state, the theory is built on a robust admissible set \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) \subseteq \bigcap_{x \in \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c) \times \{\hat{x}^d\}} \mathcal{A}(x), whose construction is a deliverable of the estimator and whose emptiness triggers an explicit safe state. A closed safe set \mathcal{S} encodes every hard constraint, and safety is enforced through a one-step reachable set \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) and a deterministic safety filter \Phi(\hat{x}) = \{a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) : \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}\}.

The first theorem is proved: if x_0 \in \mathcal{S}, the continuous estimation-error bound holds at every step, the discrete state is observed exactly, the disturbance satisfies w_t \in W almost surely, and the controller selects a_t \in \Phi(\hat{x}_t), then \mathbb{P}[\forall t \ge 0,\ x_t \in \mathcal{S}] = 1. The theorem is explicitly conditional. It asserts almost-sure invariance, not deterministic invariance, because the disturbance hypothesis is almost-sure. It asserts no liveness, deadline satisfaction, throughput, or optimality. Recursive feasibility is then shown to be a nontrivial condition: an explicit construction demonstrates that \Phi(\hat{x}) can be empty whenever auxiliary load is positive and the storage estimate is near depletion, so no filter can guarantee safety from every safe state under every disturbance sequence.

Two viability margins are constructed. For the energy subsystem, the margin

\mathcal{S}^\circ_B = \bigl\{B : \varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\overline{L} - \underline{P}) \le B \le B^{\max} - \varepsilon_B - \Delta t\,\overline{P}\bigr\}

is proved non-empty if and only if B^{\max} \ge 2\varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\overline{L} - \underline{P} + \overline{P}), which in eclipse reduces to B^{\max} \ge 2\varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\overline{L} + \overline{P}). The condition \overline{L} - \underline{P} > 0, used in earlier formulations to identify when net drain is possible, is a precondition for the problem to be non-trivial and is neither necessary nor sufficient for the margin to exist. For the thermal subsystem, the margin

\mathcal{S}^\circ_T = \bigl\{T : T \le T^{\max} - \varepsilon_T - \Delta T_{\mathrm{load}} - \Delta T_{\mathrm{disc}}\bigr\}

is proved non-empty whenever T^{\max} > T_{\mathrm{env}} + \varepsilon_T + \Delta T_{\mathrm{load}} + \Delta T_{\mathrm{disc}}, under the hypothesis Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T^{\max}) > \overline{Q}, which states that passive cooling at the temperature limit exceeds the maximum heating load. The discretization error is bounded by |e_{\mathrm{disc}}| \le (\Delta t^2 / 2) \sup |g'(T) g(T)|, which has units of temperature; the earlier expression e_{\mathrm{disc}} \Delta t / C^{\mathrm{th}} is dimensionally inconsistent and is not used.

The coupled margin \mathcal{S}^\circ = \mathcal{S}^\circ_B \cap \mathcal{S}^\circ_T is controlled-invariant under a margin filter \Phi^\circ(\hat{x}) = \{a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) : \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ\}, but only conditionally: the proof requires that at every admissible state there exists one common action that is safe in both subsystems simultaneously. This common-action hypothesis is a deliverable, not an assumption, and its failure certifies that no margin exists for the declared uncertainty. The margin filter resolves a circularity in earlier formulations, where the safety filter \Phi checks \mathcal{R} \subseteq \mathcal{S} but invariance of \mathcal{S}^\circ was asserted from the definition of the margin. The implemented validator is sound not because the filter has closed graph, which is an upper-semicontinuity property about convergent sequences and does not imply stability of individual actions under perturbations of the estimate, but because the reachable set is enclosed in interval arithmetic and acceptance requires the enclosure to lie inside \mathcal{S}.

The architecture separates intelligence from constraint enforcement. The validator is the sole component inside the trust boundary; it is deterministic, total, and sound with respect to the model. The estimator, a fixed-priority baseline, a constrained optimizer, and an optional large language model (LLM) planner all lie outside the trust boundary and can only propose actions that the validator accepts or rejects. Rejected proposals trigger a deterministic fallback that also passes through the validator; rejection of the fallback is logged as a feasibility failure rather than silently absorbed. The LLM interface is typed, schema-validated, and bounded by a token budget, and its latency, cost, rejection rate, and fallback frequency are recorded as experimental quantities. The LLM carries no theoretical guarantee and no safety authority.

Three claims remain as open obligations rather than results. The energy-aware schedulability obligation requires a necessary condition, a sufficient condition, and a proof that they coincide for a restricted single-node deterministic model with preemptive scheduling, constant power, and a storage-saturation cap of the form \int_{t_1}^{t_2} P^{\mathrm{gen}}(t)\,dt + B(t_1) \le B^{\max} added to the demand-bound test, with B(t_1) defined by the schedule rather than assumed given. The online performance obligation must compare against a stationary policy rather than the offline clairvoyant optimum, because drift-plus-penalty yields a time-average bound of the form \text{penalty} \le \text{penalty}^*_{\mathrm{stat}} + B/V with queue backlog O(V), and mean-rate stability cannot enforce hard bounds at every instant. The fault-tolerance obligation begins with an independent per-step failure model and corrects the correlated case: substituting an effective failure probability into the independent redundancy formula is conservative only for r = 1 and underestimates failure for r > 1 under common cause, because a common-cause event fails all replicas simultaneously; the correct treatment is a two-state nominal-degraded Markov model in which the degraded state contributes an indicator rather than a binomial to the availability bound, and in which the recovery rate enters through an explicit handoff delay.

The work claims a proved conditional safety theorem, a proved recursive-feasibility caveat, proved non-emptiness conditions for the energy and thermal margins, a conditional coupled-margin result whose common-action hypothesis is a deliverable, interval-arithmetic soundness as the justification for the implemented validator, and a ledger of open obligations with corrected comparators. It does not claim schedulability conditions that have not been proved, approximation or competitive bounds that have not been derived, physical safety beyond the model, benefit from LLM assistance, or flight readiness. The first executable artifact constructs the smallest state space in which the filter can be run, exhibits the recursive-feasibility failure, and demonstrates the margin restoring feasibility. Only after that artifact behaves as the theorem predicts does the work extend outward to schedulability, online performance, and fault tolerance, each with its own proof obligation and its own experimental test.

1. Problem Statement and Scope

This work develops a mathematical theory of energy-aware scheduling for a distributed computing infrastructure operating under coupled energy, thermal, availability, and communication constraints. The setting is a network of N computing nodes, each with time-varying generation, finite storage, thermal dynamics, and intermittent faults, serving a mixed-criticality workload with release times, deadlines, and resource requirements. The central question is not whether a simulation can be built, but which scheduling policies can be proved to preserve hard physical constraints, and what performance can be guaranteed when future generation is unknown.

The dissertation is organized around a theorem ledger. Each mathematical claim carries an explicit proof status. No claim is cited as a result before its proof is complete. Theorem 1 is proved under stated hypotheses. The viability margins for energy and thermal subsystems are proved for restricted models, with their non-emptiness conditions stated as inequalities. The coupled case is stated conditionally, because the existence of a common action that is safe in both subsystems is not automatic. The remaining claims — schedulability, online performance, and fault tolerance — are stated as obligations with named hypotheses, comparators, and uncertainty models. The optional large language model (LLM) component is a proposal generator evaluated empirically; it carries no theoretical guarantee and no safety authority.

2. Mathematical Setting

2.1 State Decomposition

Let the state space be X = X^c \times X^d, where

X^c \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n_c}, \qquad X^d = \{0,1\}^N \times \mathcal{Q} \times \mathcal{G}.

The continuous component x^c = (B, T, C) contains stored energy B \in \mathbb{R}^N, temperature T \in \mathbb{R}^N, and available computing capacity C \in \mathbb{R}^N. The discrete component x^d = (A, Q, \mathcal{N}) contains the availability vector A \in \{0,1\}^N, the task queue Q \in \mathcal{Q}, and the communication graph \mathcal{N} \in \mathcal{G}. The metric on X is defined by

d(x, \hat{x}) = \begin{cases} \|x^c - \hat{x}^c\| & \text{if } x^d = \hat{x}^d, \\ +\infty & \text{otherwise.} \end{cases}

The Euclidean norm is not used on the discrete component, because it is not a metric on \{0,1\}^N \times \mathcal{Q} \times \mathcal{G} in any physically meaningful way. The discrete component is assumed to be observed exactly. If the discrete component is uncertain, the reachable set must be enlarged over all consistent discrete states, which is a different and harder problem and is not addressed here.

The estimator produces \hat{x}_t \in X satisfying

x_t^d = \hat{x}_t^d, \qquad \|x_t^c - \hat{x}_t^c\| \le \varepsilon, \qquad \varepsilon \ge 0.

The first equality is exact discrete observation. The second is the continuous estimation-error bound. Both are hypotheses, not consequences. If either fails, the theorems below make no claim.

2.2 Actions, Admissibility, and Robust Admissibility

Let U \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p be the action space. The action a_t \in U assigns pending tasks to nodes at time t, subject to instantaneous power and resource limits. The admissible action map is \mathcal{A} : X \rightrightarrows U. Because the controller uses \hat{x}_t, not x_t, the admissibility check must be robust against the estimation error. Define the robust admissible set

\hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) = \bigl\{a \in U : a \in \mathcal{A}(x) \ \text{for every } x \in \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x})\bigr\}.

The set \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) is the set of actions that are admissible for every state consistent with the estimate. It is generally smaller than \mathcal{A}(\hat{x}), and it can be empty. The construction of \hat{\mathcal{A}} is a deliverable of the estimator design, not an assumption of the safety theorem. When \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) = \emptyset, no action is available that is robustly admissible, and the system enters the safe state described in Section 8.5.

2.3 Disturbances and Transition

Let W \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m be a compact disturbance set. The disturbance w_t \in W represents generation forecast error, thermal-environment variation, and unmodeled loads. The transition map is

f : X \times U \times W \to X, \qquad x_{t+1} = f(x_t, a_t, w_t),

with a_t \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}_t). The disturbance process (w_t)_{t \ge 0} is adapted to a filtration (\mathcal{F}_t)_{t \ge 0}, with w_t \in W almost surely for all t. The deterministic case is recovered by taking W to be a singleton.

Assumption 2.1 (Continuity). The continuous component of f, viewed as a map f^c : X^c \times X^d \times U \times W \to X^c, is continuous on X^c \times X^d \times U \times W.

Assumption 2.2 (Compactness). W is compact, and \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) is compact for every \hat{x}.

Assumption 2.3 (Closed graph of \hat{\mathcal{A}}). The set \{(\hat{x}, a) : a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x})\} is closed in X \times U.

2.4 Safe Set

The safe set \mathcal{S} \subseteq X is closed and is the intersection of the storage constraint \mathcal{S}_B = \{x : 0 \le B_i \le B_i^{\max}\}, the power constraint \mathcal{S}_P = \{x : P_i^{\mathrm{comp}} + P_i^{\mathrm{aux}} \le P_i^{\mathrm{gen}} + P_i^{\mathrm{dis}}\}, the thermal constraint \mathcal{S}_T = \{x : T_i \le T_i^{\max}\}, the resource constraint \mathcal{S}_C = \{x : \text{assigned work} \le C_i\}, and the availability constraint \mathcal{S}_A = \{x : A_i = 0 \Rightarrow \text{no work assigned to } i\}. The scheduler's task is to keep the true state inside \mathcal{S} for all time, not merely on average.

2.5 Reachable Set and Safety Filter

For an estimate \hat{x} and action a, the one-step reachable set is

\mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) = \bigl\{ f(x, a, w) : x^c \in \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c), \ x^d = \hat{x}^d, \ w \in W \bigr\}.

The discrete component is fixed at \hat{x}^d, because it is observed exactly. The safety filter is

\Phi(\hat{x}) = \bigl\{ a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) : \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S} \bigr\}.

The filter accepts exactly those robustly admissible actions whose uncertainty-worst-case successor lies inside the safe set.

3. Theorem 1: Soundness of the One-Step Safety Filter

Theorem 1. Let Assumptions 2.1–2.3 hold. Suppose x_0 \in \mathcal{S}. Suppose that for every t \ge 0, x_t^d = \hat{x}_t^d, \|x_t^c - \hat{x}_t^c\| \le \varepsilon, w_t \in W almost surely, and the controller selects a_t \in \Phi(\hat{x}_t). Then

\mathbb{P}\bigl[\forall t \ge 0,\ x_t \in \mathcal{S}\bigr] = 1.

Proof. By induction on t along almost every sample path. For t = 0, the hypothesis gives x_0 \in \mathcal{S}. Suppose x_t \in \mathcal{S}, x_t^d = \hat{x}_t^d, and \|x_t^c - \hat{x}_t^c\| \le \varepsilon. Then x_t \in \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}_t^c) \times \{\hat{x}_t^d\}. Since a_t \in \Phi(\hat{x}_t), the definition of \Phi gives \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}_t, a_t) \subseteq \mathcal{S}. Since w_t \in W almost surely,

x_{t+1} = f(x_t, a_t, w_t) \in \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}_t, a_t) \subseteq \mathcal{S}

almost surely. The induction closes on the almost-sure event \bigcap_{t \ge 0} \{w_t \in W\}, which has probability one. \square

Remark 3.1 (Almost-sure, not deterministic). The conclusion is almost-sure because the hypothesis on w_t is almost-sure. If w_t \in W holds for every sample path, the conclusion holds for every sample path. The validator enforces the deterministic version when the disturbance set is treated as a hard bound.

Remark 3.2 (Conditionality). The theorem proves safety conditional on three hypotheses: the continuous estimation-error bound holds at every step, the discrete state is observed exactly, and the filter is non-empty at every step. None of these is derived. The first is a property of the estimator. The second is a property of the sensing and communication layer. The third is recursive feasibility, and it can fail.

Remark 3.3 (Relative soundness). Soundness is with respect to f, \mathcal{S}, and W. If the physical system deviates from f, or if \mathcal{S} omits a hazard, the theorem does not protect against it. Numerical rounding is enclosed by evaluating \mathcal{R} in interval arithmetic (Section 6). Physical model error is addressed by model validation, not by the theorem.

Remark 3.4 (No liveness, no optimality). The theorem asserts invariance of \mathcal{S}. It says nothing about task completion, deadline satisfaction, throughput, or energy efficiency.

4. Recursive Feasibility and Viability

The recursive-feasibility hypothesis of Theorem 1 is falsifiable. It is falsified by simple physical configurations.

Proposition 1. There exist admissible model parameters and estimates \hat{x} with \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c) \times \{\hat{x}^d\} \cap \mathcal{S} \neq \emptyset such that \Phi(\hat{x}) = \emptyset.

Proof (construction). Consider a single node with storage B, auxiliary load P_{\mathrm{aux}} > 0, zero generation, and no admissible computing load. The storage dynamics give B_{t+1} = B_t - \Delta t\, P_{\mathrm{aux}} for every action. If \hat{B} < \varepsilon_B + \Delta t\, P_{\mathrm{aux}}, then every x^c \in \mathcal{B}_{\varepsilon_B}(\hat{x}^c) has B < \Delta t\, P_{\mathrm{aux}}, hence every successor has B_{t+1} < 0. No action keeps the successor inside \mathcal{S}. Therefore \Phi(\hat{x}) = \emptyset. \square

The construction does not require pathological parameters. It requires only that the storage be near depletion, that auxiliary load be positive, and that the estimation error be non-negligible. In orbit, auxiliary load is always positive (housekeeping, thermal control), so the failure mode is generic.

4.1 Viability Kernel

The viability kernel is defined by an iterated operator, not by a one-step lookahead. Let

\mathrm{Viab}_0(\mathcal{S}) = \mathcal{S}, \qquad \mathrm{Viab}_{k+1}(\mathcal{S}) = \bigl\{x \in \mathcal{S} : \exists a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x),\ \forall w \in W,\ f(x, a, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S})\bigr\},

and define

\mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}) = \bigcap_{k \ge 0} \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}).

Under Assumptions 2.1–2.3, each \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}) is closed, and the intersection is closed and non-empty if \mathcal{S} is compact and viable at some point.

Lemma 4.1. If x_0 \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}), then there exists a policy \pi such that x_t \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}) for all t \ge 0.

Proof. If x_0 \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}) = \bigcap_k \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}), then x_0 \in \mathrm{Viab}_{k+1}(\mathcal{S}) for every k. By definition, there exists a_0 \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x_0) such that f(x_0, a_0, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}) for every k and every w \in W. Hence f(x_0, a_0, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}). The induction then closes. \square

The viability margin defined in Section 5 is a computable inner approximation of \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}), constructed by explicit inequalities rather than by iterating the kernel operator.

5. Energy Viability Margin

5.1 Single-Node Dynamics

For a single node, the storage dynamics are

B_{t+1} = B_t + \Delta t\bigl(P_{\mathrm{gen}} - L(a)\bigr),

with generation P_{\mathrm{gen}} \in [\underline{P}, \overline{P}], total load L(a) = P_{\mathrm{comp}}(a) + P_{\mathrm{aux}} \in [0, \overline{L}], and estimation error |B_t - \hat{B}_t| \le \varepsilon_B. The storage constraint is 0 \le B_t \le B^{\max}.

5.2 Margin and Non-Emptiness

Proposition 2. Define the energy margin

\mathcal{S}^\circ_B = \bigl\{B : \varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\overline{L} - \underline{P}) \le B \le B^{\max} - \varepsilon_B - \Delta t\,(\overline{P} - \underline{P})\bigr\}.

Then \mathcal{S}^\circ_B is non-empty if and only if

B^{\max} \ge 2\varepsilon_B + \Delta t\,(\overline{L} - \underline{P} + \overline{P} - \underline{P}) = 2\varepsilon_B + \Delta t\,(\overline{L} + \overline{P} - 2\underline{P}).

In eclipse, \underline{P} = 0, and the condition reduces to B^{\max} \ge 2\varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\overline{L} + \overline{P}).

Proof. Non-emptiness of \mathcal{S}^\circ_B is the inequality

\varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\overline{L} - \underline{P}) \le B^{\max} - \varepsilon_B - \Delta t(\overline{P} - \underline{P}).

Rearranging yields the stated condition. \square

Remark 5.1 (Corrected hypothesis). The condition \overline{L} - \underline{P} > 0, used in earlier versions, identifies when net drain is possible. That is a precondition for the scheduling problem to be non-trivial, not a condition for the margin to be non-empty. The correct non-emptiness condition is the capacity inequality above. In eclipse, the margin must be large enough to cover the worst-case load during the eclipse plus the worst-case generation uncertainty on both sides of the estimate.

5.3 Invariance

Proposition 3. Let \hat{B} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ_B. Let a be any action with L(a) \in [0, \overline{L}]. Then

\mathcal{R}_B(\hat{B}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}_B.

Proof. With \hat{B} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ_B,

\hat{B} - \varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\underline{P} - \overline{L}) \ge \varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\overline{L} - \underline{P}) - \varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\underline{P} - \overline{L}) = 0,

and

\hat{B} + \varepsilon_B + \Delta t\,\overline{P} \le B^{\max} - \varepsilon_B - \Delta t(\overline{P} - \underline{P}) + \varepsilon_B + \Delta t\,\overline{P} = B^{\max} + \Delta t\,\underline{P} \le B^{\max}

provided \underline{P} \le 0. If \underline{P} > 0, the upper bound is strengthened by replacing \overline{P} - \underline{P} with \overline{P} in the margin definition, yielding the tighter form

\mathcal{S}^\circ_B = \bigl\{B : \varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\overline{L} - \underline{P}) \le B \le B^{\max} - \varepsilon_B - \Delta t\,\overline{P}\bigr\}.

The reachable interval is contained in [0, B^{\max}]. \square

Remark 5.2 (Hypothesis on the estimate). The correct hypothesis is \hat{B} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ_B directly, not the weaker condition \mathcal{B}_{\varepsilon_B}(\hat{B}) \cap \mathcal{S}^\circ_B \neq \emptyset. The weaker condition gives only \hat{B} \ge \varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\overline{L} - \underline{P}) - \varepsilon_B = \Delta t(\overline{L} - \underline{P}), which does not suffice for the lower endpoint. The estimate must lie inside the margin, not merely near it.

6. Thermal Viability Margin

6.1 Thermal Dynamics and Discretization

The thermal dynamics are

C^{\mathrm{th}} \dot{T} = Q^{\mathrm{in}}(a) - Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T),

with heat rejection

Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T) = h(T - T_{\mathrm{env}}) + \varepsilon_{\mathrm{rad}} \sigma (T^4 - T_{\mathrm{env}}^4).

The explicit Euler scheme

T_{t+1} = T_t + \Delta t\, g(T_t), \qquad g(T) = \frac{Q^{\mathrm{in}}(a) - Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T)}{C^{\mathrm{th}}},

has local truncation error

e_{\mathrm{disc}} = \frac{\Delta t^2}{2} \ddot{T} + O(\Delta t^3), \qquad \ddot{T} = g'(T) g(T).

The bound is

|e_{\mathrm{disc}}| \le \frac{\Delta t^2}{2} \sup_{T \in [T_{\mathrm{env}}, T^{\max}]} \bigl|g'(T) g(T)\bigr|.

This expression has units of temperature. The equivalent form in terms of Q^{\mathrm{out}} is

|e_{\mathrm{disc}}| \le \frac{\Delta t^2}{2 (C^{\mathrm{th}})^2} \sup \left| \frac{dQ^{\mathrm{out}}}{dT} \cdot \bigl(Q^{\mathrm{in}} - Q^{\mathrm{out}}\bigr) \right|.

The earlier form e_{\mathrm{disc}} \Delta t / C^{\mathrm{th}} is dimensionally inconsistent and is not used.

6.2 Margin and Non-Emptiness

Proposition 4. Suppose Q^{\mathrm{out}} is non-decreasing on [T_{\mathrm{env}}, T^{\max}] and Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T^{\max}) > \overline{Q}, where \overline{Q} = \sup_a Q^{\mathrm{in}}(a). Define

\mathcal{S}^\circ_T = \bigl\{T : T \le T^{\max} - \varepsilon_T - \Delta T_{\mathrm{load}} - \Delta T_{\mathrm{disc}}\bigr\},

with \Delta T_{\mathrm{load}} = \Delta t\, \overline{Q} / C^{\mathrm{th}} and \Delta T_{\mathrm{disc}} = \sup |e_{\mathrm{disc}}|. Then \mathcal{S}^\circ_T is non-empty whenever

T^{\max} > T_{\mathrm{env}} + \varepsilon_T + \Delta T_{\mathrm{load}} + \Delta T_{\mathrm{disc}}.

Proof. The condition Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T^{\max}) > \overline{Q} ensures that at T = T^{\max}, even under maximum heating, the net heat flow is negative in the continuous model, so T cannot increase past T^{\max}. The margin accounts for the discretization error and the estimation error. Non-emptiness follows from the inequality. \square

Remark 6.1 (The hypothesis Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T^{\max}) > \overline{Q} is used). This condition is the physical statement that the cooling system can reject more heat than the maximum heating load at the temperature limit. Without it, the thermal margin may be empty, and no guarantee is available.

7. Coupled Margin and Controlled Invariance

7.1 Coupled Margin

Proposition 5 (coupled margin, conditional). Let \mathcal{S}^\circ_B and \mathcal{S}^\circ_T be the margins of Propositions 2 and 4, computed with disturbance bounds that include the coupling terms (thermal dissipation of computing energy, temperature dependence of storage efficiency). If at every \hat{x} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ_B \cap \mathcal{S}^\circ_T there exists a common action

a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) \quad \text{with} \quad \mathcal{R}_B(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ_B \ \text{and} \ \mathcal{R}_T(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ_T,

then \mathcal{S}^\circ = \mathcal{S}^\circ_B \cap \mathcal{S}^\circ_T is controlled-invariant.

Proof. The existence of a common action is the hypothesis. Under it, the successor lies in both \mathcal{S}^\circ_B and \mathcal{S}^\circ_T, hence in their intersection. \square

Remark 7.1 (The common action is not automatic). The intersection of two margins may be controlled-invariant under the intersection of the two filters even when no single action is safe in both subsystems. The proposition requires the stronger condition of a common action. This condition is a deliverable, not an assumption. Its failure certifies that no margin exists for the declared uncertainty, and therefore that no guarantee is available without reducing uncertainty or load.

7.2 Margin Filter

The safety filter \Phi checks \mathcal{R} \subseteq \mathcal{S}, not \mathcal{R} \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ. The margin does not enter the filter's acceptance condition. To connect the margin to the filter, define the margin filter

\Phi^\circ(\hat{x}) = \bigl\{a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) : \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ\bigr\}.

Proposition 6. If \hat{x} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ and a \in \Phi^\circ(\hat{x}), then for every x \in \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c) \times \{\hat{x}^d\} and every w \in W, f(x, a, w) \in \mathcal{S}^\circ.

Proof. Direct from the definition of \Phi^\circ: \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ, and f(x, a, w) \in \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a). \square

Corollary 6.1 (Safety under the margin filter). Under the hypotheses of Theorem 1 with \Phi replaced by \Phi^\circ, the trajectory remains in \mathcal{S}^\circ \subseteq \mathcal{S} almost surely, provided \Phi^\circ(\hat{x}_t) \neq \emptyset for every t.

Remark 7.2 (The circularity is resolved). The earlier version asserted \mathcal{R} \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ by definition of the margin, but the filter \Phi only checks \mathcal{R} \subseteq \mathcal{S}. The margin filter \Phi^\circ is the correct object. Its recursive feasibility is a separate obligation.

8. Interval Arithmetic and Soundness of the Implemented Validator

The validator cannot evaluate \mathcal{R} exactly in floating point. It evaluates an interval enclosure [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a).

Lemma 8.1 (Interval inclusion). If [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a) is computed by interval arithmetic over the box \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c) \times \{\hat{x}^d\} \times [W], then \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a).

Proof. Direct from the inclusion property of interval arithmetic, applied componentwise. \square

Lemma 8.2 (Soundness of the implemented validator). If the validator accepts a only when [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}, then every accepted action satisfies \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}.

Proof. By Lemma 8.1, \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a). If [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}, then \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}. \square

Remark 8.1 (The closed-graph lemma does not justify floating point). An earlier version used the closed graph of \Phi to justify floating-point implementation. Closed graph is an upper-semicontinuity property: it says that if (\hat{x}_k, a_k) \to (\hat{x}, a) with a_k \in \Phi(\hat{x}_k), then a \in \Phi(\hat{x}). It does not say that if a \in \Phi(\hat{x}) and \hat{x}' is close to \hat{x}, then a \in \Phi(\hat{x}'). The latter is false in general. The correct justification for floating-point implementation is Lemma 8.2, which is soundness of the interval evaluation.

9. Architecture

9.1 Components and Trust Boundary

The system is decomposed into five layers.

The environment layer generates (w_t)_{t \ge 0}: generation trace, thermal-environment trace, and fault trace.

The plant layer implements f: storage dynamics, thermal dynamics, capacity dynamics, availability transitions, communication graph evolution, and task completion.

The estimator layer produces \hat{x}_t and the error bound \varepsilon. It also produces the robust admissible set \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}_t). The estimator is outside the trust boundary. Its error bound and its robust admissible set are hypotheses of Theorem 1, not consequences.

The scheduler layer contains proposal generators: a fixed-priority baseline, a constrained optimizer, and the optional LLM planner. Each proposes an action a_t^{\mathrm{prop}}.

The validator layer implements \Phi and \Phi^\circ. It is the only component inside the trust boundary. It is deterministic, total, and sound with respect to the model.

The trust boundary is a one-way gate: proposals flow in, accept/reject decisions flow out. No proposal generator has a path to modify the validator, the safe set, or the disturbance bounds.

9.2 Validator Specification

The validator is a function

\text{Validate} : (\hat{x}, a) \mapsto \{\text{accept}, \text{reject}\}

implemented as a conjunction of predicate checks.

1. Schema check. The action conforms to the declared schema.
2. Reference check. Every task ID and node ID exists in the snapshot.
3. Robust admissibility check. a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}).
4. Resource check. Assigned work does not exceed C_i for any node.
5. Power check. Instantaneous load does not exceed worst-case available power.
6. Energy check. [\mathcal{R}_B](\hat{x}, a) \subseteq [0, B^{\max}] in interval arithmetic.
7. Thermal check. [\mathcal{R}_T](\hat{x}, a) \subseteq (-\infty, T^{\max}] in interval arithmetic.
8. Criticality check. Critical tasks are assigned to nodes whose availability is guaranteed for the task duration.

The validator returns accept only if all checks pass. Otherwise it returns reject with the failing predicate identified. The validator is total: it terminates on every input because each check is a finite arithmetic evaluation. Its complexity is O(N \cdot |Q|) in the number of nodes and queue length.

9.3 LLM Interface and Validation Pipeline

The LLM interface is a five-stage pipeline.

1. Snapshot construction. A typed subset of the state is serialized.
2. Prompt assembly. A fixed prompt template is combined with the snapshot. The prompt declares the permitted action space and the hard constraints, but it does not expose the validator's internals.
3. Inference. The LLM produces a plan in a declared JSON schema, subject to a token budget.
4. Parse and schema-validate. The plan is parsed; schema violations trigger fallback.
5. Semantic and constraint validation. The validator checks the plan against the model. Acceptance yields an action; rejection triggers fallback.

Fallback is the deterministic constrained optimizer, which itself passes through the validator. If the fallback is rejected, the system enters the explicit safe state.

9.4 Safe State

The safe state is the zero-compute, minimal-auxiliary configuration. It is selected when no action is admissible. Entering the safe state is a logged event, not a success. The simulation counts feasibility failures separately from constraint violations.

9.5 Model Versioning

The plant model f, the safe set \mathcal{S}, and the disturbance set W are versioned. Every theorem, proposition, and experiment names the model version it applies to.

10. Algorithms

10.1 Baseline

The fixed-priority baseline selects tasks by priority and deadline subject to immediate feasibility. It is transparent but weak; it anchors comparisons and provides a fallback.

10.2 Constrained Optimizer

For small instances, a mixed-integer linear program is solved exactly. Variables: z_{j,i,t} \in \{0,1\} for task j on node i at time t. Objective:

\max \sum_{j,i,t} v_j z_{j,i,t} - \lambda_E \sum_{i,t} P_{i,t}^{\mathrm{comp}} \Delta t - \lambda_D \sum_j D_j.

Constraints include assignment uniqueness, resource capacity, storage dynamics without clipping, instantaneous power limits, thermal limits, and lateness definitions. For instances with N \le 5 and |Q| \le 20, the MILP is solved exactly by branch-and-bound.

For larger instances, Lagrangian relaxation decomposes the problem by node. The dual function is an upper bound on the primal optimum for every non-negative multiplier vector (weak duality). The best upper bound is the minimum over multipliers. The duality gap for the mixed-integer problem is not guaranteed to be zero; it is reported empirically.

10.3 Lyapunov Drift-Plus-Penalty

Virtual queues for storage and thermal constraints are defined by

Q_{B_i}(t+1) = \max\bigl[Q_{B_i}(t) - B_i(t) + B_i^{\min}, 0\bigr] + \bigl[B_i^{\max} - B_i(t)\bigr]_+,

Q_{T_i}(t+1) = \max\bigl[Q_{T_i}(t) - (T_i^{\max} - T_i(t)), 0\bigr] + \bigl[T_i(t) - T_i^{\max}\bigr]_+.

At each step, the algorithm selects the action minimizing

\Delta(t) + V\, \mathbb{E}\bigl[\text{penalty}(t) \mid Q(t)\bigr],

where \Delta(t) is the quadratic drift. The resulting time-average bound is

\limsup_{T \to \infty} \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \mathbb{E}[\text{penalty}(t)] \le \text{penalty}^*_{\mathrm{stat}} + \frac{B}{V},

with B a constant depending on maximum arrival and service rates, and all virtual queues mean-rate stable. The comparator \text{penalty}^*_{\mathrm{stat}} is the optimum over stationary policies satisfying the same constraints. This is not a bound against the offline clairvoyant optimum. Mean-rate stability is a time-average statement; it does not imply B_i(t) \ge 0 or T_i(t) \le T_i^{\max} at every t. Safety comes from the filter, not from Lyapunov drift.

10.4 Thermal-Aware Heuristic

At each step, compute the thermal slack h_i(T_i) = T_i^{\max} - T_i. Rank tasks by the score

s_j = \frac{v_j}{e_j} \cdot \frac{h_{i(j)}(T_{i(j)})}{T^{\max}_{i(j)}},

where i(j) is the node with the largest thermal slack that can accommodate j. Assign tasks in decreasing order of s_j, subject to energy and resource constraints. The barrier condition is checked after assignment.

10.5 ED-H Schedulability Test

For a single node with deterministic generation P^{\mathrm{gen}}(t), the ED-H test checks, for every interval [t_1, t_2],

\sum_{j : r_j \ge t_1,\ d_j \le t_2} e_j \le \int_{t_1}^{t_2} P^{\mathrm{gen}}(t)\, dt + B(t_1) - B^{\min},

together with the saturation cap

\int_{t_1}^{t_2} P^{\mathrm{gen}}(t)\, dt + B(t_1) \le B^{\max}.

The second inequality is the saturation cap; without it, excess generation is curtailed and cannot be used. The term B(t_1) is defined by the schedule, not assumed given. The test is necessary and sufficient only under the assumptions of preemptive scheduling, constant power during execution, and instantaneous power limits satisfied at every instant. Without these assumptions, ED-H gives a sufficient condition, not a necessary and sufficient one.

10.6 Complexity

The MILP has O(N |Q| T) binary variables, making exact solution exponential in the worst case. The Lagrangian decomposition reduces the per-iteration cost to O(N \cdot \mathrm{poly}(|Q|, T)). The Lyapunov algorithm is O(N |Q|) per step. The validator is O(N |Q|) per call. The LLM inference is bounded by the token budget.

11. Fault Tolerance

11.1 Independent Failures

Assume each node fails independently at each time step with probability p, and a failed node recovers after a geometrically distributed time with mean 1/q. A critical task of duration d with redundancy r (executed simultaneously on r distinct nodes) completes if at least one of the r nodes remains available for the entire duration. If recovery is instantaneous (q = \infty), the completion probability is

P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) = 1 - \bigl[1 - (1-p)^d\bigr]^r.

For finite recovery time, a failed node hands the task to a spare after a delay \delta \approx 1/q. The completion probability satisfies the recursion

P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) = (1-p)^d + \sum_{k=1}^{d} p (1-p)^{k-1} \cdot P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r-1, d - k - \delta),

with P_{\mathrm{crit}}(0, d) = 0. The recursion is solved numerically.

11.2 Correlated Failures

Common-cause failures are modeled by a two-state Markov chain with states \{\text{nominal}, \text{degraded}\}. In the nominal state, nodes fail independently with probability p_0. In the degraded state, all nodes fail simultaneously with probability p_1 \gg p_0. Transition probabilities are p_{01} and p_{10}. The steady-state probabilities are

\pi_{\mathrm{nom}} = \frac{p_{10}}{p_{01} + p_{10}}, \qquad \pi_{\mathrm{deg}} = \frac{p_{01}}{p_{01} + p_{10}}.

The completion probability of a critical task is

P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) = \pi_{\mathrm{nom}} \cdot P_{\mathrm{ind}}(r, d, p_0) + \pi_{\mathrm{deg}} \cdot P_{\mathrm{deg}}(r, d, p_1),

where P_{\mathrm{ind}} is the independent-failure formula with p_0, and P_{\mathrm{deg}} is the completion probability in the degraded state. If the degraded state fails all nodes simultaneously, then P_{\mathrm{deg}} = 0 for any task that requires at least one node during the degraded interval.

Remark 11.1 (Substituting p_{\mathrm{eff}} is not conservative for r > 1). Let p_{\mathrm{eff}} = \pi_{\mathrm{nom}} p_0 + \pi_{\mathrm{deg}} p_1. Substituting p_{\mathrm{eff}} into the independent-failure formula is conservative for r = 1 by convexity, but it underestimates the failure probability for r > 1 under common cause. Under a common-cause event, all r replicas fail simultaneously, so redundancy provides no benefit. The independent formula with p_{\mathrm{eff}} incorrectly assigns independent failures to the replicas. The correct treatment is the two-state model above.

11.3 Availability Bound

Under the independent model, the number of available nodes R(t) is \mathrm{Binomial}(N, 1-p). The probability that at least r nodes are available is

\Pr[R(t) \ge r] = \sum_{k=r}^{N} \binom{N}{k} (1-p)^k p^{N-k}.

Under the correlated model, the availability bound is

\Pr[R(t) \ge r] = \pi_{\mathrm{nom}} \sum_{k=r}^{N} \binom{N}{k} (1-p_0)^k p_0^{N-k} + \pi_{\mathrm{deg}} \cdot \mathbf{1}[r = 0].

The second term is an indicator, not a binomial, because the degraded state fails all nodes simultaneously. The earlier version's binomial in the degraded state contradicted the "all nodes fail simultaneously" description.

11.4 Fault-Tolerance Guarantee (Statement of T4)

T4. Under the independent failure model with parameters (N, p, q, \delta), for every critical task with duration d and redundancy r, the completion probability satisfies P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) \ge 1 - \alpha whenever r \ge r^*(\alpha, d, p, \delta). The threshold r^* is computed from the recursion in Section 11.1. The extension to correlated failures replaces the independent model with the two-state model and provides a bound via the holding-time distribution of the degraded state. The independence assumption is declared, not assumed to hold in orbit.

12. Theorem Ledger and Open Obligations

T1. Soundness of the one-step safety filter. Status: proved. Theorem 1, under Assumptions 2.1–2.3 and the estimation hypotheses. Conclusion: almost-sure invariance of \mathcal{S}.

T1′. Controlled invariance of the margin. Status: proved conditionally. Proposition 6 and Corollary 6.1, under the hypothesis that the margin filter is non-empty at every step and that a common action exists for the coupled system (Proposition 5).

T2. Energy-aware schedulability. Status: objective. For a restricted model — single node, deterministic generation, no thermal coupling, preemptive scheduling, constant power — characterize the set of task systems for which a feasible schedule exists. Deliverables: necessary condition, sufficient condition, and proof that the two coincide for the restricted class. The saturation cap and the definition of B(t_1) from the schedule are required. The general multi-node coupled case is expected to be intractable for exact characterization.

T3. Online performance bound. Status: objective. Fix one algorithm, one comparator, and one uncertainty model. The comparator is a stationary policy, not the offline clairvoyant optimum. Required derivation: an explicit bound of the form

\text{penalty} \le \text{penalty}^*_{\mathrm{stat}} + \frac{B}{V},

with queue backlog O(V), under the assumption that a feasible stationary policy exists. If the bound cannot be derived for the coupled problem, the thesis reports the bound for the decoupled problem and states the coupling as an open obligation.

T4. Fault-tolerance guarantee. Status: objective. The independent model is stated, and the correlated model is stated. The recursion and the threshold r^* are the deliverables.

13. Scope of Claims

The dissertation claims Theorem 1 and its corollaries as proved, conditional on their stated hypotheses. It claims the recursive-feasibility caveat of Proposition 1 as a proved negative result. It claims the energy and thermal margins of Propositions 2 and 4 as proved sufficient conditions for recursive feasibility in the restricted single-subsystem case. It claims the coupled margin of Proposition 5 as a conditional result whose common-action hypothesis is a deliverable, not an assumption. It claims the interval-arithmetic soundness of Lemma 8.2 as the justification for the implemented validator.

It does not claim schedulability conditions that have not been proved. It does not claim approximation or competitive bounds that have not been derived. It does not claim that the validator is physically safe, only that it is sound with respect to a stated model. It does not claim that the LLM helps. It does not claim flight readiness. It does not claim that the model captures every physical hazard.

The first executable artifact tests T1 and Proposition 1: it constructs the smallest state space in which the filter can be run, it exhibits the recursive-feasibility failure, and it demonstrates the margin restoring feasibility. Only after that artifact behaves as the theorem predicts does the work move outward to schedulability, online bounds, and fault tolerance.

14. Conclusion

STAR-BRICK 01, Version 1.1, is a theorem-driven research program with corrected proofs, repaired hypotheses, and explicit open obligations. Theorem 1 is proved under hypotheses that are stated and that can fail. The recursive-feasibility caveat is a proved negative result. The energy and thermal margins are proved sufficient conditions with non-emptiness inequalities. The coupled case is conditional on a common-action hypothesis that is a deliverable. The interval-arithmetic validator is the sole justification for floating-point implementation. The fault-tolerance model is corrected for common cause, and the Lyapunov comparator is corrected to a stationary policy. The work does not claim that a star-powered civilization is feasible, nor that an LLM can manage it. It claims a specific class of scheduling problems admits a sound safety filter, that recursive feasibility is nontrivial, and that a viability margin restores feasibility under declared assumptions. From that foundation, the work extends outward to schedulability, online performance, and fault tolerance, each with its own proof obligation and its own experimental test.

Appendices

Appendix A — Mathematical Foundations

A.1 State Decomposition and Metric

The state space is X = X^c \times X^d with

X^c \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n_c}, \qquad X^d = \{0,1\}^N \times \mathcal{Q} \times \mathcal{G},

where X^c contains stored energy B, temperature T, and computing capacity C, and X^d contains the availability vector A, the task queue Q, and the communication graph \mathcal{N}. The discrete component is not a subset of any Euclidean space in a physically meaningful way. The metric on X is defined by

d(x, \hat{x}) = \begin{cases} \|x^c - \hat{x}^c\|_2 & \text{if } x^d = \hat{x}^d, \\ +\infty & \text{otherwise.} \end{cases}

This metric is not induced by any norm on X, but it is a valid metric: it is symmetric, vanishes only when x = \hat{x}, and satisfies the triangle inequality on each branch. The discrete component is treated as observed exactly. If the discrete component is uncertain, the reachable set must be enlarged over all consistent discrete states, which changes the problem and is not addressed here.

A.2 Regularity Assumptions

Assumption A.1 (Continuity). The map f^c : X^c \times X^d \times U \times W \to X^c, obtained by projecting f onto its continuous component, is continuous on its domain.

Assumption A.2 (Compactness). W \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m is compact. For every \hat{x} \in X, the robust admissible set \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) is compact.

Assumption A.3 (Closed graph of \hat{\mathcal{A}}). The set \{(\hat{x}, a) : a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x})\} is closed in X \times U.

Assumption A.4 (Disturbance process). (w_t)_{t \ge 0} is adapted to a filtration (\mathcal{F}_t)_{t \ge 0} on a probability space (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P}), with w_t \in W almost surely for all t.

Under A.1–A.3, the reachable set

\mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) = \bigl\{ f(x, a, w) : x^c \in \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c),\ x^d = \hat{x}^d,\ w \in W \bigr\}

is compact for every (\hat{x}, a), because it is the continuous image of the compact set \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c) \times \{\hat{x}^d\} \times \{a\} \times W.

A.3 Robust Admissible Set

Because the controller uses \hat{x}_t, not x_t, admissibility must be robust against the estimation error. Define

\hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) = \bigl\{a \in U : a \in \mathcal{A}(x) \ \text{for every } x \in \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c) \times \{\hat{x}^d\}\bigr\}.

The set \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) is the set of actions admissible for every state consistent with the estimate. It is generally smaller than \mathcal{A}(\hat{x}), and it can be empty. When \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) = \emptyset, no robustly admissible action exists, and the system enters the safe state. This is a modeling requirement, not a theorem. Its construction is a deliverable of the estimator design.

A.4 Interval Arithmetic and Inclusion

An interval is [a] = [\underline{a}, \overline{a}] with \underline{a} \le \overline{a}. The fundamental theorem of interval arithmetic states: if g : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R} is a rational function and [x] \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n is an interval box, then g([x]) \supseteq \{g(x) : x \in [x]\}. This is the inclusion property.

The mean value form

g(x) \in g(m) + g'([x])([x] - m), \qquad m \in [x],

reduces the over-approximation from O(\text{width}) to O(\text{width}^2) for differentiable g. The dependency problem — repeated variables treated independently — is the principal source of over-approximation. For the storage dynamics, B_t appears once on the right-hand side, so the dependency problem is mild. For the thermal dynamics, T_t appears in both linear and quartic cooling terms, so the mean value form is used.

A.5 Viability Kernels

The viability kernel is defined by an iterated operator. Let

\mathrm{Viab}_0(\mathcal{S}) = \mathcal{S}, \qquad \mathrm{Viab}_{k+1}(\mathcal{S}) = \bigl\{x \in \mathcal{S} : \exists a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x),\ \forall w \in W,\ f(x, a, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S})\bigr\},

and set

\mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}) = \bigcap_{k \ge 0} \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}).

Lemma A.5. Under Assumptions A.1–A.3, each \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}) is closed, and \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}) is closed.

Proof. By induction on k. For k = 0, \mathrm{Viab}_0(\mathcal{S}) = \mathcal{S}, which is closed by hypothesis. Suppose \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}) is closed. Let (x_j) be a sequence in \mathrm{Viab}_{k+1}(\mathcal{S}) with x_j \to x. For each j, there exists a_j \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x_j) with f(x_j, a_j, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}) for every w. By compactness of \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x_j) in a neighborhood of x, the sequence (a_j) has a convergent subsequence a_{j_\ell} \to a. By Assumption A.3, a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x). By continuity of f and closedness of \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}), f(x, a, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}) for every w. Hence x \in \mathrm{Viab}_{k+1}(\mathcal{S}), which is therefore closed. The intersection of closed sets is closed. \square

Lemma A.6 (Maximal invariant subset). If x_0 \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}), then there exists a policy \pi such that x_t \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}) for all t \ge 0.

Proof. Suppose x_0 \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}) = \bigcap_k \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}). Then x_0 \in \mathrm{Viab}_{k+1}(\mathcal{S}) for every k. By definition, for each k there exists a_0^{(k)} \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x_0) with f(x_0, a_0^{(k)}, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}) for every w \in W. By compactness of \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x_0), the sequence (a_0^{(k)}) has a convergent subsequence with limit a_0 \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x_0). By continuity of f and closedness of each \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}), f(x_0, a_0, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}_k(\mathcal{S}) for every k and every w. Hence f(x_0, a_0, w) \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}). The induction then closes at each subsequent step. \square

The earlier one-step definition of the viability kernel does not support this induction. The iterated definition does.

A.6 Discrete-Time Control Barrier Functions

A function h : X^c \to \mathbb{R} is a discrete-time control barrier function for the set \mathcal{S}_h = \{x^c : h(x^c) \ge 0\} if there exists \gamma \in (0,1] such that for every x^c \in \mathcal{S}_h, there exists a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(x) with

h(f^c(x, a, w)) \ge (1 - \gamma) h(x^c) \qquad \forall w \in W.

Lemma A.7. If h is a discrete-time CBF and h(x_0^c) \ge 0, then h(x_t^c) \ge 0 for all t \ge 0 under any policy that selects a CBF-satisfying action at each step.

Proof. By induction. If h(x_t^c) \ge 0, then h(x_{t+1}^c) \ge (1-\gamma) h(x_t^c) \ge 0. \square

The CBF condition is sufficient for forward invariance. It is not necessary. Smaller \gamma admits more actions but recovers more slowly from near the boundary.

A.7 Lyapunov Drift and Virtual Queues

Let Q(t) \in \mathbb{R}_+^k be virtual queues with

Q_i(t+1) = \max\bigl[Q_i(t) - b_i(t), 0\bigr] + a_i(t).

The quadratic Lyapunov function is L(t) = \tfrac{1}{2} \sum_i Q_i(t)^2, and the one-step drift is

\Delta(t) = \mathbb{E}\bigl[L(t+1) - L(t) \mid Q(t)\bigr].

The standard drift bound is

\Delta(t) \le B + \sum_i Q_i(t)\, \mathbb{E}\bigl[a_i(t) - b_i(t) \mid Q(t)\bigr],

with B = \tfrac{1}{2} \sum_i (a_i^{\max 2} + b_i^{\max 2}). The drift-plus-penalty algorithm minimizes

\Delta(t) + V\, \mathbb{E}\bigl[\text{penalty}(t) \mid Q(t)\bigr].

The resulting time-average bound is

\limsup_{T \to \infty} \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \mathbb{E}[\text{penalty}(t)] \le \text{penalty}^*_{\mathrm{stat}} + \frac{B}{V},

where \text{penalty}^*_{\mathrm{stat}} is the optimum over stationary policies satisfying the same constraints, and all virtual queues are mean-rate stable. This is a time-average statement. It does not imply B_i(t) \ge 0 or T_i(t) \le T_i^{\max} at every t. Hard bounds are enforced by the filter, not by Lyapunov drift. The comparator is a stationary policy, not the offline clairvoyant optimum.

A.8 Demand Bound and ED-H

For a task system with release times r_j, deadlines d_j, and execution requirements e_j, the demand bound over [t_1, t_2] is

\mathrm{dbf}(t_1, t_2) = \sum_{j : r_j \ge t_1,\ d_j \le t_2} e_j.

Under energy constraints, the ED-H schedulability test requires

\mathrm{dbf}(t_1, t_2) \le \int_{t_1}^{t_2} P^{\mathrm{gen}}(t)\, dt + B(t_1) - B^{\min},

together with the saturation cap

\int_{t_1}^{t_2} P^{\mathrm{gen}}(t)\, dt + B(t_1) \le B^{\max}.

The second inequality prevents curtailment of excess generation. The term B(t_1) is defined by the schedule, not assumed given. The test is necessary and sufficient only under preemptive scheduling, constant power during execution, and instantaneous power limits satisfied at every instant.

Appendix B — Proofs

B.1 Theorem 1

Theorem 1. Let Assumptions A.1–A.4 hold. Suppose x_0 \in \mathcal{S}. Suppose that for every t \ge 0: x_t^d = \hat{x}_t^d, \|x_t^c - \hat{x}_t^c\| \le \varepsilon, w_t \in W almost surely, and the controller selects a_t \in \Phi(\hat{x}_t). Then

\mathbb{P}\bigl[\forall t \ge 0,\ x_t \in \mathcal{S}\bigr] = 1.

Proof. Let \Omega_W = \bigcap_{t \ge 0} \{w_t \in W\}. By Assumption A.4, \mathbb{P}[\Omega_W] = 1. Fix \omega \in \Omega_W. The proof is an induction on t along \omega.

Base. x_0 \in \mathcal{S} by hypothesis.

Step. Suppose x_t \in \mathcal{S}, x_t^d = \hat{x}_t^d, and \|x_t^c - \hat{x}_t^c\| \le \varepsilon. Then x_t \in \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}_t^c) \times \{\hat{x}_t^d\}. Since a_t \in \Phi(\hat{x}_t), the definition of \Phi gives \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}_t, a_t) \subseteq \mathcal{S}. Since w_t \in W, x_{t+1} = f(x_t, a_t, w_t) \in \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}_t, a_t) \subseteq \mathcal{S}.

The induction closes for every \omega \in \Omega_W. \square

Remark B.1.1 (Almost-sure, not deterministic). The conclusion is almost-sure because the hypothesis on w_t is almost-sure. If w_t \in W holds for every sample path, the conclusion holds for every sample path. The validator enforces the deterministic version when the disturbance set is treated as a hard bound.

Remark B.1.2 (Conditionality). The theorem is conditional on three hypotheses: the continuous estimation-error bound holds at every step, the discrete state is observed exactly, and the filter is non-empty at every step. None is derived. The first is a property of the estimator. The second is a property of the sensing and communication layer. The third is recursive feasibility, and it can fail (Proposition 1).

Remark B.1.3 (Relative soundness). Soundness is with respect to f, \mathcal{S}, and W. If the physical system deviates from f, or if \mathcal{S} omits a hazard, the theorem does not protect against it. Numerical rounding is enclosed by evaluating \mathcal{R} in interval arithmetic (Lemma B.8.1). Physical model error is addressed by model validation.

B.2 Proposition 1 (Recursive Feasibility Failure)

Proposition 1. There exist admissible model parameters and an estimate \hat{x} with \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c) \times \{\hat{x}^d\} \cap \mathcal{S} \neq \emptyset such that \Phi(\hat{x}) = \emptyset.

Proof (construction). Consider a single node with storage B, auxiliary load P_{\mathrm{aux}} > 0, zero generation P_{\mathrm{gen}} \equiv 0, and no admissible computing load. The storage dynamics give

B_{t+1} = B_t - \Delta t\, P_{\mathrm{aux}}

for every action. Choose \hat{B} with \hat{B} < \varepsilon_B + \Delta t\, P_{\mathrm{aux}}. For every x^c \in \mathcal{B}_{\varepsilon_B}(\hat{x}^c), B \le \hat{B} + \varepsilon_B < \Delta t\, P_{\mathrm{aux}}, hence B_{t+1} < 0. Thus no successor lies in \mathcal{S}_B, and since \mathcal{A}(\hat{x}) = \{a_0\}, \Phi(\hat{x}) = \emptyset. \square

The construction uses only positive auxiliary load and non-negligible estimation error. In orbit, auxiliary load is always positive, so the failure mode is generic.

B.3 Proposition 2 (Energy Viability Margin, Corrected)

For a single node, the storage dynamics are

B_{t+1} = B_t + \Delta t\bigl(P_{\mathrm{gen}} - L(a)\bigr),

with P_{\mathrm{gen}} \in [\underline{P}, \overline{P}], L(a) \in [0, \overline{L}], and estimation error |B_t - \hat{B}_t| \le \varepsilon_B. The storage constraint is 0 \le B_t \le B^{\max}.

Proposition 2. Define

\mathcal{S}^\circ_B = \bigl\{B : \varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\overline{L} - \underline{P}) \le B \le B^{\max} - \varepsilon_B - \Delta t\, \overline{P}\bigr\}.

Then \mathcal{S}^\circ_B is non-empty if and only if

B^{\max} \ge 2\varepsilon_B + \Delta t\,(\overline{L} - \underline{P} + \overline{P}).

In eclipse, \underline{P} = 0, and the condition reduces to

B^{\max} \ge 2\varepsilon_B + \Delta t\,(\overline{L} + \overline{P}).

Proof. Non-emptiness of \mathcal{S}^\circ_B is the inequality

\varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\overline{L} - \underline{P}) \le B^{\max} - \varepsilon_B - \Delta t\, \overline{P}.

Rearranging,

2\varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\overline{L} - \underline{P}) + \Delta t\, \overline{P} \le B^{\max},

which is the stated condition. \square

Remark B.3.1 (Corrected hypothesis). The condition \overline{L} - \underline{P} > 0, which identifies when net drain is possible, is a precondition for the scheduling problem to be non-trivial, not a condition for the margin to be non-empty. The correct non-emptiness condition is the capacity inequality above. In eclipse, the margin must cover the worst-case load during the eclipse plus the generation uncertainty on both sides of the estimate.

B.4 Proposition 3 (Energy Invariance)

Proposition 3. Let \hat{B} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ_B. Let a be any action with L(a) \in [0, \overline{L}]. Then

\mathcal{R}_B(\hat{B}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}_B.

Proof. With \hat{B} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ_B,

\hat{B} - \varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\underline{P} - \overline{L}) \ge \varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\overline{L} - \underline{P}) - \varepsilon_B + \Delta t(\underline{P} - \overline{L}) = 0,

and

\hat{B} + \varepsilon_B + \Delta t\, \overline{P} \le B^{\max} - \varepsilon_B - \Delta t\, \overline{P} + \varepsilon_B + \Delta t\, \overline{P} = B^{\max}.

The reachable interval is contained in [0, B^{\max}]. \square

Remark B.4.1 (Corrected hypothesis). The correct hypothesis is \hat{B} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ_B directly, not the weaker condition \mathcal{B}_{\varepsilon_B}(\hat{B}) \cap \mathcal{S}^\circ_B \neq \emptyset. The weaker condition gives only \hat{B} \ge \Delta t(\overline{L} - \underline{P}), which does not suffice for the lower endpoint.

Remark B.4.2 (Recursive feasibility of the margin). For the margin to be recursively feasible, we require that if B_t \in \mathcal{S}^\circ_B, then the estimate \hat{B}_t lies in the region where the filter is non-empty. Since \hat{B}_t \in [B_t - \varepsilon_B, B_t + \varepsilon_B], the additional condition

\Delta t\,(\overline{L} - \underline{L}_{\min}) \ge \varepsilon_B

is required, where \underline{L}_{\min} is the minimum achievable load. When L_{\min} = 0, this is \Delta t\, \overline{L} \ge \varepsilon_B: the estimation error must not exceed the maximum per-step energy consumption. If this condition fails, the margin is not recursively feasible, and no guarantee is available without reducing uncertainty or load.

B.5 Proposition 4 (Thermal Viability Margin, Corrected)

The thermal dynamics are

C^{\mathrm{th}} \dot{T} = Q^{\mathrm{in}}(a) - Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T),

with heat rejection

Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T) = h(T - T_{\mathrm{env}}) + \varepsilon_{\mathrm{rad}}\, \sigma (T^4 - T_{\mathrm{env}}^4).

The explicit Euler scheme

T_{t+1} = T_t + \Delta t\, g(T_t), \qquad g(T) = \frac{Q^{\mathrm{in}}(a) - Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T)}{C^{\mathrm{th}}},

has local truncation error

e_{\mathrm{disc}} = \frac{\Delta t^2}{2} \ddot{T} + O(\Delta t^3), \qquad \ddot{T} = g'(T)\, g(T).

The bound is

|e_{\mathrm{disc}}| \le \frac{\Delta t^2}{2} \sup_{T \in [T_{\mathrm{env}}, T^{\max}]} |g'(T)\, g(T)|.

Equivalently, in terms of Q^{\mathrm{out}},

|e_{\mathrm{disc}}| \le \frac{\Delta t^2}{2 (C^{\mathrm{th}})^2} \sup \left| \frac{dQ^{\mathrm{out}}}{dT} \cdot (Q^{\mathrm{in}} - Q^{\mathrm{out}}) \right|.

The units are temperature. The earlier form e_{\mathrm{disc}} \Delta t / C^{\mathrm{th}} is dimensionally inconsistent and is not used.

Proposition 4. Suppose Q^{\mathrm{out}} is non-decreasing on [T_{\mathrm{env}}, T^{\max}] and Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T^{\max}) > \overline{Q}, where \overline{Q} = \sup_a Q^{\mathrm{in}}(a). Define

\mathcal{S}^\circ_T = \bigl\{T : T \le T^{\max} - \varepsilon_T - \Delta T_{\mathrm{load}} - \Delta T_{\mathrm{disc}}\bigr\},

with \Delta T_{\mathrm{load}} = \Delta t\, \overline{Q} / C^{\mathrm{th}} and \Delta T_{\mathrm{disc}} = \sup |e_{\mathrm{disc}}|. Then \mathcal{S}^\circ_T is non-empty whenever

T^{\max} > T_{\mathrm{env}} + \varepsilon_T + \Delta T_{\mathrm{load}} + \Delta T_{\mathrm{disc}}.

Proof. The condition Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T^{\max}) > \overline{Q} ensures that at T = T^{\max}, the net heat flow Q^{\mathrm{in}} - Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T^{\max}) < 0 in the continuous model, so T cannot increase past T^{\max}. The margin subtracts the discretization error and the load-induced temperature rise from T^{\max}. Non-emptiness follows from the inequality. \square

Remark B.5.1 (Units). The temperature error e_{\mathrm{disc}} has units of temperature. The correct margin is \Delta T_{\mathrm{disc}} = \sup |e_{\mathrm{disc}}|, not e_{\mathrm{disc}} \Delta t / C^{\mathrm{th}}. The earlier expression rescales an already-temperature-valued quantity by a time-to-heat-capacity ratio, which is dimensionally incorrect.

Remark B.5.2 (The hypothesis Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T^{\max}) > \overline{Q} is used). This condition states that the cooling system at the temperature limit rejects more heat than the maximum heating load. Without it, the thermal margin may be empty.

B.6 Proposition 5 (Coupled Margin, Conditional)

Proposition 5. Let \mathcal{S}^\circ_B and \mathcal{S}^\circ_T be the margins of Propositions 2 and 4, computed with disturbance bounds that include the coupling terms. If at every \hat{x} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ_B \cap \mathcal{S}^\circ_T there exists a common action

a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) \quad \text{with} \quad \mathcal{R}_B(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ_B \ \text{and} \ \mathcal{R}_T(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ_T,

then \mathcal{S}^\circ = \mathcal{S}^\circ_B \cap \mathcal{S}^\circ_T is controlled-invariant.

Proof. The existence of a common action is the hypothesis. Under it, the successor lies in both \mathcal{S}^\circ_B and \mathcal{S}^\circ_T, hence in their intersection. \square

Remark B.6.1 (The common action is not automatic). The intersection of two margins may be controlled-invariant under the intersection of the two filters even when no single action is safe in both subsystems. The proposition requires the stronger condition of a common action. Its failure certifies that no margin exists for the declared uncertainty, and therefore that no guarantee is available without reducing uncertainty or load.

B.7 Proposition 6 (Margin Filter Invariance)

The safety filter \Phi checks \mathcal{R} \subseteq \mathcal{S}, not \mathcal{R} \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ. To connect the margin to a filter, define

\Phi^\circ(\hat{x}) = \bigl\{a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}) : \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ\bigr\}.

Proposition 6. If \hat{x} \in \mathcal{S}^\circ and a \in \Phi^\circ(\hat{x}), then for every x \in \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c) \times \{\hat{x}^d\} and every w \in W, f(x, a, w) \in \mathcal{S}^\circ.

Proof. Direct from the definition of \Phi^\circ: \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ, and f(x, a, w) \in \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a). \square

Corollary B.7.1. Under the hypotheses of Theorem 1 with \Phi replaced by \Phi^\circ, the trajectory remains in \mathcal{S}^\circ \subseteq \mathcal{S} almost surely, provided \Phi^\circ(\hat{x}_t) \neq \emptyset for every t.

Remark B.7.2 (The circularity is resolved). The earlier version asserted \mathcal{R} \subseteq \mathcal{S}^\circ "by definition of the margin," but the filter \Phi only checks \mathcal{R} \subseteq \mathcal{S}. The margin filter \Phi^\circ is the correct object. Its recursive feasibility is a separate obligation, established by the common-action hypothesis of Proposition 5 and the margin construction of Propositions 2 and 4.

B.8 Interval Soundness

Lemma B.8.1 (Interval inclusion). If [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a) is computed by interval arithmetic over the box \mathcal{B}_\varepsilon(\hat{x}^c) \times \{\hat{x}^d\} \times [W], then \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a).

Proof. Direct from the inclusion property of interval arithmetic, applied componentwise. \square

Lemma B.8.2 (Soundness of the implemented validator). If the validator accepts a only when [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}, then every accepted action satisfies \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}.

Proof. By Lemma B.8.1, \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a). If [\mathcal{R}](\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}, then \mathcal{R}(\hat{x}, a) \subseteq \mathcal{S}. \square

Remark B.8.3. The earlier version used the closed graph of \Phi to justify floating-point implementation. Closed graph is an upper-semicontinuity property: it says that if (\hat{x}_k, a_k) \to (\hat{x}, a) with a_k \in \Phi(\hat{x}_k), then a \in \Phi(\hat{x}). It does not say that if a \in \Phi(\hat{x}) and \hat{x}' is close to \hat{x}, then a \in \Phi(\hat{x}'). The latter is false in general. The correct justification for floating-point implementation is Lemma B.8.2.

B.9 Discretization Error Bound

Lemma B.9.1. For the explicit Euler scheme T_{t+1} = T_t + \Delta t\, g(T_t) applied to \dot{T} = g(T) with g \in C^2, the local truncation error satisfies

|e_{\mathrm{disc}}| \le \frac{\Delta t^2}{2} \sup_{T \in [T_{\mathrm{env}}, T^{\max}]} |g'(T)\, g(T)|.

Proof. Taylor expansion of the exact solution:

T(t + \Delta t) = T(t) + \Delta t\, g(T(t)) + \frac{\Delta t^2}{2} \ddot{T}(t) + O(\Delta t^3),

with \ddot{T} = g'(T)\, g(T). Subtracting the Euler step yields the bound. \square

Lemma B.9.2 (Equivalent form in terms of Q^{\mathrm{out}}). With g(T) = (Q^{\mathrm{in}} - Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T))/C^{\mathrm{th}},

|e_{\mathrm{disc}}| \le \frac{\Delta t^2}{2 (C^{\mathrm{th}})^2} \sup_{T \in [T_{\mathrm{env}}, T^{\max}]} \left| \frac{dQ^{\mathrm{out}}}{dT}(T) \cdot \bigl(Q^{\mathrm{in}} - Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T)\bigr) \right|.

Proof. Compute g'(T) = -Q'^{\mathrm{out}}(T)/C^{\mathrm{th}} and g(T) = (Q^{\mathrm{in}} - Q^{\mathrm{out}}(T))/C^{\mathrm{th}}. Substituting into g'(T) g(T) yields the stated expression. \square

Lemma B.9.3 (Stability of the explicit scheme). For the linearized cooling dynamics \dot{T} = -(T - T_{\mathrm{env}})/\tau with \tau = C^{\mathrm{th}}/h, the explicit Euler scheme is stable if \Delta t < 2\tau.

Proof. The amplification factor is 1 - \Delta t/\tau. Stability requires |1 - \Delta t/\tau| \le 1, which gives 0 \le \Delta t/\tau \le 2, i.e., \Delta t \le 2\tau. \square

Appendix C — Architecture

C.1 Trust Boundary

The system is decomposed into five layers: environment, plant, estimator, scheduler, and validator. The validator is the only component inside the trust boundary. It is deterministic, total, and sound with respect to the model. The estimator, the fixed-priority baseline, the constrained optimizer, and the optional LLM planner lie outside the trust boundary and can only propose actions. The trust boundary is a one-way gate: proposals flow in, accept/reject decisions flow out. No proposal generator has a path to modify the validator, the safe set, or the disturbance bounds.

C.2 State Schema

The state is serialized as a JSON object with top-level fields:

```
state:
  nodes: [ { id, B, B_max, T, T_max, C, A, N } ]
  queue: [ { id, w, e, d, p, r, critical } ]
  time: t
  horizon: T_end
  constraints:
    energy: { bounds: [0, B_max], epsilon_B, epsilon_P }
    thermal: { bounds: [-inf, T_max], epsilon_T, e_disc }
    availability: { unavailable: [ids] }
```

The snapshot passed to the LLM is a subset: node states, pending tasks, permitted action space, and hard constraints. The LLM receives no internal state of the validator and no access to the disturbance set.

C.3 Validator Predicates

The validator is a function \text{Validate} : (\hat{x}, a) \mapsto \{\text{accept}, \text{reject}\} implemented as a conjunction of predicate checks:

1. Schema check: the action conforms to the declared schema.
2. Reference check: every task ID and node ID exists.
3. Robust admissibility check: a \in \hat{\mathcal{A}}(\hat{x}).
4. Resource check: assigned work does not exceed C_i.
5. Power check: instantaneous load does not exceed worst-case available power.
6. Energy check: [\mathcal{R}_B](\hat{x}, a) \subseteq [0, B^{\max}] in interval arithmetic.
7. Thermal check: [\mathcal{R}_T](\hat{x}, a) \subseteq (-\infty, T^{\max}] in interval arithmetic.
8. Criticality check: critical tasks assigned to nodes whose availability is guaranteed for the task duration.

The validator returns accept only if all checks pass. Its complexity is O(N \cdot |Q|) in nodes and queue length.

C.4 LLM Interface

The LLM interface is a five-stage pipeline:

1. Snapshot construction: a typed subset of state is serialized.
2. Prompt assembly: a fixed template combined with the snapshot; the prompt declares the permitted action space and hard constraints but not the validator internals.
3. Inference: the LLM produces a plan in a declared JSON schema, subject to a token budget.
4. Parse and schema-validate: schema violations trigger fallback.
5. Semantic and constraint validation: the validator checks the plan. Acceptance yields an action; rejection triggers fallback.

C.5 Fallback and Safe State

Fallback is the deterministic constrained optimizer, which passes through the validator. If the fallback is rejected, the system enters the explicit safe state: zero-compute, minimal-auxiliary configuration. Entering the safe state is a logged event. The simulation counts feasibility failures separately from constraint violations, because a feasibility failure means the margin was exhausted, not that the filter was unsound.

C.6 Model Versioning

The plant model f, the safe set \mathcal{S}, and the disturbance set W are versioned. Every theorem, proposition, and experiment names the model version it applies to. A change in the plant model invalidates prior guarantees until they are re-proved for the new version.

Appendix D — Algorithms

D.1 Mixed-Integer Linear Program

Variables: z_{j,i,t} \in \{0,1\} for task j on node i at time t; B_{i,t} \ge 0; T_{i,t} (linearized); D_j \ge 0 for lateness. Objective:

\max \sum_{j,i,t} v_j z_{j,i,t} - \lambda_E \sum_{i,t} P_{i,t}^{\mathrm{comp}} \Delta t - \lambda_D \sum_j D_j.

Constraints:

\sum_{i,t} z_{j,i,t} \le 1 \ \forall j, \qquad \sum_j w_j z_{j,i,t} \le C_i \ \forall i, t,

B_{i,t+1} = B_{i,t} + \Delta t(P_{i,t}^{\mathrm{gen}} - P_{i,t}^{\mathrm{comp}} - P_{i,t}^{\mathrm{aux}}),

0 \le B_{i,t} \le B_i^{\max}, \quad P_{i,t}^{\mathrm{comp}} + P_{i,t}^{\mathrm{aux}} \le P_{i,t}^{\mathrm{gen}} + P_{i,t}^{\mathrm{dis}},

T_{i,t+1} \le T_i^{\max}, \quad D_j \ge t_{\mathrm{completion}}(j) - d_j.

For N \le 5 and |Q| \le 20, the MILP is solved exactly by branch-and-bound.

D.2 Lagrangian Relaxation

Relaxing the energy and thermal coupling constraints with multipliers \lambda \ge 0, \mu \ge 0, the Lagrangian is

\mathcal{L}(z, \lambda, \mu) = \sum_j v_j z_j - \lambda^T g_E(z) - \mu^T g_T(z).

The dual function g(\lambda, \mu) = \max_{z \in \mathcal{Z}} \mathcal{L}(z, \lambda, \mu) decomposes by node, and the single-node subproblem is solved exactly by dynamic programming. Dual variables are updated by subgradient ascent:

\lambda^{(k+1)} = \bigl[\lambda^{(k)} + \alpha_k g_E(z^{(k)})\bigr]_+, \qquad \mu^{(k+1)} = \bigl[\mu^{(k)} + \alpha_k g_T(z^{(k)})\bigr]_+.

For maximization, g(\lambda, \mu) is an upper bound on the primal optimum for every \lambda, \mu \ge 0 (weak duality). The best upper bound is \min_{\lambda, \mu \ge 0} g(\lambda, \mu). The duality gap for the mixed-integer problem is not guaranteed to be zero; it is reported empirically.

D.3 Lyapunov Drift-Plus-Penalty

Virtual queues:

Q_{B_i}(t+1) = \max\bigl[Q_{B_i}(t) - B_i(t) + B_i^{\min}, 0\bigr] + \bigl[B_i^{\max} - B_i(t)\bigr]_+,

Q_{T_i}(t+1) = \max\bigl[Q_{T_i}(t) - (T_i^{\max} - T_i(t)), 0\bigr] + \bigl[T_i(t) - T_i^{\max}\bigr]_+.

At each step, select the action minimizing

\Delta(t) + V\, \mathbb{E}[\text{penalty}(t) \mid Q(t)].

The resulting time-average bound is

\limsup_{T \to \infty} \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \mathbb{E}[\text{penalty}(t)] \le \text{penalty}^*_{\mathrm{stat}} + \frac{B}{V},

with B a constant and all virtual queues mean-rate stable. The comparator \text{penalty}^*_{\mathrm{stat}} is the optimum over stationary policies satisfying the same constraints, not the offline clairvoyant optimum. Mean-rate stability is a time-average statement; it does not imply B_i(t) \ge 0 or T_i(t) \le T_i^{\max} at every t. Hard bounds are enforced by the filter.

D.4 Thermal-Aware Heuristic

At each step, compute thermal slack h_i(T_i) = T_i^{\max} - T_i. Rank tasks by

s_j = \frac{v_j}{e_j} \cdot \frac{h_{i(j)}(T_{i(j)})}{T^{\max}_{i(j)}},

where i(j) is the node with the largest thermal slack that can accommodate j. Assign in decreasing order of s_j, subject to energy and resource constraints. The CBF condition is checked after assignment.

D.5 ED-H Schedulability Test (Corrected)

For a single node with deterministic generation P^{\mathrm{gen}}(t), the ED-H test checks, for every interval [t_1, t_2],

\sum_{j : r_j \ge t_1,\ d_j \le t_2} e_j \le \int_{t_1}^{t_2} P^{\mathrm{gen}}(t)\, dt + B(t_1) - B^{\min},

together with the saturation cap

\int_{t_1}^{t_2} P^{\mathrm{gen}}(t)\, dt + B(t_1) \le B^{\max}.

The saturation cap prevents curtailment of excess generation. The term B(t_1) is defined by the schedule, not assumed given. The test is necessary and sufficient only under preemptive scheduling, constant power during execution, and instantaneous power limits satisfied at every instant. Without these assumptions, ED-H gives a sufficient condition, not a necessary and sufficient one.

D.6 Complexity

The MILP has O(N |Q| T) binary variables, so exact solution is exponential in the worst case. Lagrangian decomposition reduces the per-iteration cost to O(N \cdot \mathrm{poly}(|Q|, T)). The Lyapunov algorithm is O(N |Q|) per step. The validator is O(N |Q|) per call. LLM inference is bounded by the token budget.

Appendix E — Fault Tolerance

E.1 Independent Failure Model

Each node fails independently at each time step with probability p, and a failed node recovers after a geometrically distributed time with mean 1/q. A critical task of duration d with redundancy r completes if at least one of the r nodes remains available for the duration. If recovery is instantaneous (q = \infty), the completion probability is

P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) = 1 - \bigl[1 - (1-p)^d\bigr]^r.

For finite recovery time, a failed node hands the task to a spare after a delay \delta \approx 1/q. The completion probability satisfies the recursion

P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) = (1-p)^d + \sum_{k=1}^{d} p (1-p)^{k-1} \cdot P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r-1, d - k - \delta),

with P_{\mathrm{crit}}(0, d) = 0. The recursion is solved numerically. The rate q enters through \delta.

E.2 Correlated Failure Model

Common-cause failures are modeled by a two-state Markov chain with states \{\text{nominal}, \text{degraded}\}. In nominal state, nodes fail independently with probability p_0. In degraded state, all nodes fail simultaneously. Transition probabilities are p_{01} and p_{10}, with steady-state probabilities

\pi_{\mathrm{nom}} = \frac{p_{10}}{p_{01} + p_{10}}, \qquad \pi_{\mathrm{deg}} = \frac{p_{01}}{p_{01} + p_{10}}.

The completion probability of a critical task is

P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) = \pi_{\mathrm{nom}} \cdot P_{\mathrm{ind}}(r, d, p_0) + \pi_{\mathrm{deg}} \cdot P_{\mathrm{deg}}(r, d),

where P_{\mathrm{ind}} is the independent-failure formula with p_0, and P_{\mathrm{deg}} is the completion probability in the degraded state. If the degraded state fails all nodes simultaneously, then P_{\mathrm{deg}} = 0 for any task requiring at least one node during the degraded interval.

Remark E.2.1 (Substituting p_{\mathrm{eff}} is not conservative for r > 1). Let p_{\mathrm{eff}} = \pi_{\mathrm{nom}} p_0 + \pi_{\mathrm{deg}} p_1. Substituting p_{\mathrm{eff}} into the independent-failure formula is conservative for r = 1 by convexity, but underestimates the failure probability for r > 1 under common cause. Under a common-cause event, all replicas fail simultaneously, so redundancy provides no benefit. The independent formula with p_{\mathrm{eff}} incorrectly assigns independent failures to the replicas. The correct treatment is the two-state model above.

E.3 Availability Bound

Under the independent model, the number of available nodes R(t) is \mathrm{Binomial}(N, 1-p). The probability that at least r nodes are available is

\Pr[R(t) \ge r] = \sum_{k=r}^{N} \binom{N}{k} (1-p)^k p^{N-k}.

Under the correlated model,

\Pr[R(t) \ge r] = \pi_{\mathrm{nom}} \sum_{k=r}^{N} \binom{N}{k} (1-p_0)^k p_0^{N-k} + \pi_{\mathrm{deg}} \cdot \mathbf{1}[r = 0].

The second term is an indicator, not a binomial, because the degraded state fails all nodes simultaneously. The earlier binomial in the degraded state contradicted the "all nodes fail simultaneously" description.

E.4 Redundancy Sizing

For a prescribed risk level \alpha, the threshold r^*(\alpha, d, p, \delta) is the smallest r such that P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) \ge 1 - \alpha, with P_{\mathrm{crit}} given by the recursion in Section E.1. Under correlated failures, the same threshold is computed with the two-state model, and the bound is conservative in the sense that it assumes the worst-case holding-time distribution for the degraded state.

E.5 Statement of T4

T4. Under the independent failure model with parameters (N, p, q, \delta), for every critical task with duration d and redundancy r, the completion probability satisfies P_{\mathrm{crit}}(r, d) \ge 1 - \alpha whenever r \ge r^*(\alpha, d, p, \delta). The extension to correlated failures replaces the independent model with the two-state model and provides a bound via the holding-time distribution. The independence assumption is declared, not assumed to hold in orbit.